(window.__D=window.__D||{})["data/ch03.json"]={"n": 3, "title": "The Kingdom of Pontus (to AD 64)", "events": [{"id": "H2-001", "date": "-302", "date_text": "302 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Mithridates (later 'Ktistes') escapes Antigonus and seizes Kimiata", "summary": "After Antigonus the One-Eyed had Mithridates of Kios killed (302), the younger Mithridates, a companion of Demetrius, fled east with six horsemen when Antigonus, warned by a dream, planned to arrest him. He fortified a stronghold, Kimiata at the foot of the Olgassys range, gathered followers and made himself lord of Cappadocia-by-the-Sea (Pontus). This is the foundation story of the Mithridatid dynasty, which later bore the epithet Ktistes, 'the Founder', for him.", "people": ["Mithridates I Ktistes", "Antigonus I Monophthalmus", "Demetrius Poliorcetes", "Mithridates of Kios"], "sources_primary": ["Diodorus 20.111.4", "Plutarch, Demetrius 4", "Appian, Mith. 9", "Strabo 12.3.41"], "sources_modern": ["Bosworth & Wheatley 1998 (JHS 118)", "McGing 1986, ch. 1", "Erciyas 2006"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Diodorus makes him the successor of Mithridates of Kios (ruling 36 years, i.e. to 266); Plutarch calls him son of Ariobarzanes. The exact family tree and whether Ktistes was son or nephew of the Kian dynast are reconstructed differently (Bosworth & Wheatley 1998). The dream and the 'Flee, Mithridates' written in the sand by Demetrius are court legend.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kios (Gemlik)", "lat": 40.433, "lon": 29.157, "role": "seat of Mithridates of Kios, killed by Antigonus in 302"}, {"name": "Kimiata (Olgassys foothills; Ilgaz Daglari)", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 33.7, "role": "fortress used as his base; site not identified, coordinates approximate"}]}, {"id": "H2-002", "date": "-297", "date_text": "Autumn 297/296 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Epoch of the Pontic royal era", "summary": "The later coins of Mithridates VI are dated by a royal era whose year 1 began in autumn 297 BC. The same epoch is used by the Bithynian kings, so the era may mark a claim to independence from the successors of Alexander rather than a Pontic event. It gives a second possible start for the kingdom, between the flight of 302 and the royal title of 281.", "people": ["Mithridates I Ktistes"], "sources_primary": ["Dated tetradrachms of Mithridates VI (e.g. year 202 = 96/95 BC)"], "sources_modern": ["de Callatay 1997 (L'histoire des guerres mithridatiques vue par les monnaies)", "McGing 1986, ch. 1"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Whether the era is genuinely Pontic or borrowed from Bithynia is debated; it does not by itself prove a royal title in 297.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "early royal seat"}]}, {"id": "H2-003", "date": "-281", "date_text": "C. 281 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Mithridates I takes the royal title", "summary": "After Seleucus' victory at Corupedium and his murder (281), Mithridates styled himself king (basileus). The chronographer Syncellus gives the Pontic kings 218 years, i.e. 281-63 BC, which is the conventional span of the kingdom. Amaseia in the Iris gorge became the royal city.", "people": ["Mithridates I Ktistes"], "sources_primary": ["George Syncellus, Chronographia (218 years of the Pontic kings)", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 9-11", "Strabo 12.3.39"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1", "Hojte 2009 (ed.)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The kingdom's beginning is dated to 302 (the flight), 297/6 (the era) or 281 (the royal title).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "royal city"}, {"name": "Kimiata", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 33.7, "role": "first base (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H2-004", "date": "-281", "date_text": "C. 281 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Borders c. 281 BC: the first kingdom", "summary": "The early kingdom held the interior between the Halys and the Iris: Kimiatene at the foot of the Olgassys, the Phazemonitis, the Amaseia gorge and the middle Iris valley around Gaziura and Komana. It had no Greek city of its own on the coast until Amastris was handed over about 279; Sinope, Amisos, Kotyora, Kerasous and Trapezous were still independent Greek cities.", "people": ["Mithridates I Ktistes"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 12.3.39-41", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 9"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1", "Erciyas 2006, ch. 2"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The early extent is inferred; no ancient source draws it. When Amisos came under the kings, in the 3rd or early 2nd century, is unknown.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kimiatene", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 33.7, "role": "inside (west edge, approximate)"}, {"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "inside (capital)"}, {"name": "Phazemonitis", "lat": 41.143, "lon": 35.455, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Gaziura", "lat": 40.387, "lon": 36.083, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Komana Pontica", "lat": 40.355, "lon": 36.635, "role": "inside (probable)"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "outside: free Greek city"}, {"name": "Amisos", "lat": 41.3, "lon": 36.34, "role": "outside: free Greek city (status uncertain)"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.727, "role": "outside: free Greek city"}]}, {"id": "H2-005", "date": "-279", "date_text": "C. 279 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Amastris handed to Ariobarzanes", "summary": "Eumenes, who held Amastris, handed the city to Ariobarzanes son of Mithridates rather than to Heraclea, which wanted it. It was the dynasty's first Greek port on the Black Sea, west of Sinope.", "people": ["Ariobarzanes", "Eumenes (nephew of Philetairos?)"], "sources_primary": ["Memnon, FGrH 434 F 9.4"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1", "Saprykin 1997"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The identity of this Eumenes and the exact year are uncertain.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amastris (Amasra)", "lat": 41.747, "lon": 32.386, "role": "city handed over"}, {"name": "Heraclea Pontica", "lat": 41.283, "lon": 31.417, "role": "rival claimant"}]}, {"id": "H2-006", "date": "-266", "date_text": "C. 266 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Ariobarzanes king (c. 266-c. 250)", "summary": "Ariobarzanes succeeded his father. Memnon records trouble with the Galatians, the Celtic invaders settled in central Anatolia from 278/7, during his reign and that of his young son.", "people": ["Ariobarzanes of Pontus", "Galatians"], "sources_primary": ["Memnon, FGrH 434 F 16"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "His reign is dated only approximately, from about 266 to about 255 or 250.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "royal city"}]}, {"id": "H2-007", "date": "-250", "date_text": "C. 250 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Mithridates II king (c. 250-c. 220); Seleucid marriages", "summary": "Mithridates II married a Seleucid princess, Laodice, and married his daughters to Antiochus III and to the usurper Achaeus. Polybius reports that the house claimed descent from one of the seven Persians who made Darius king. With Galatian allies he helped Antiochus Hierax defeat Seleucus II at Ankyra (c. 240/239).", "people": ["Mithridates II of Pontus", "Laodice", "Antiochus III", "Achaeus", "Seleucus II", "Antiochus Hierax"], "sources_primary": ["Polybius 5.43.1-4", "Polybius 8.20.11", "Justin 27.2.10-12", "Eusebius, Chronicle"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The date of the battle of Ankyra (about 240–235) and Mithridates' part in it are debated.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "royal city"}, {"name": "Ankyra", "lat": 39.94, "lon": 32.86, "role": "battle c. 240/239"}]}, {"id": "H2-008", "date": "-220", "date_text": "220 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Mithridates II besieges Sinope; Rhodes helps the city", "summary": "Mithridates II attacked Sinope, the leading Greek city of the coast. The Rhodians sent money (140,000 drachmas) and war material, and the city held out. The episode shows the kings' long ambition for the great harbour.", "people": ["Mithridates II of Pontus"], "sources_primary": ["Polybius 4.56"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "besieged"}]}, {"id": "H2-009", "date": "-220", "date_text": "C. 220 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Mithridates III (c. 220-c. 185)", "summary": "A king Mithridates between Mithridates II and Pharnaces I is known mainly from portrait coins. Most modern king lists count him as Mithridates III.", "people": ["Mithridates III of Pontus"], "sources_primary": ["Royal tetradrachms with the portrait of a king Mithridates"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1", "de Callatay 2009 (in Hojte ed.)"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "His existence and dates rest on coins; some scholars merge him with Mithridates II. The modern numbering of the kings depends on it.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "royal city"}]}, {"id": "H2-010", "date": "-189", "date_text": "C. 189/185 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Pharnaces I king (c. 189/185-c. 160/155)", "summary": "Pharnaces I was the most aggressive of the early kings: he took Sinope and fought a four-year war against Pergamon, Bithynia and Cappadocia. He married the Seleucid princess Nysa.", "people": ["Pharnaces I of Pontus", "Nysa"], "sources_primary": ["Polybius 23.9; 24.1; 25.2", "Strabo 12.3.11"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "His accession is dated about 189 or 185, his death between about 170 and 155 (see the Athenian decree, H2-017).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "royal city"}]}, {"id": "H1-067", "date": "-183", "date_text": "After 183 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Pharnakeia founded: the end of Kotyora", "summary": "After seizing Sinope in 183 BC, Pharnaces I of Pontus founded Pharnakeia by moving in the people of Kotyora (Strabo 12.3.17). Arrian says Pharnakeia was 'formerly called Kerasous' and was itself a Sinopean colony (Periplus 16.4). Pharnakeia is generally placed at Giresun. Kotyora shrank to a village (Arrian), and Sinope's old colonial chain passed to the kings of Pontus.", "kind": "event", "people": ["Pharnaces I", "Pharnaces I of Pontus"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 12.3.17", "Arrian, Periplus 16.3–4", "Anonymous Periplus Ponti Euxini 34–35", "Arrian, Periplus 16.4", "Pliny, NH 6.11"], "sources_modern": ["Olshausen & Biller 1984, Historisch-geographische Aspekte der Geschichte des Pontischen und Armenischen Reiches", "Tabula Peutingeriana database (tp-online, no. 1539)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985 (on Kerasous and Pharnakeia)", "McGing 1986, ch. 1"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The new city is said to have taken in the people of Kotyora (Strabo) or of Kerasous (Arrian, the Anonymous Periplus), or both. Pharnakeia is usually placed at Giresun. Strabo (12.3.17) also lists a separate Kerasous, in a bay with Hermonassa east of Pharnakeia, which supports keeping the two names apart; whether Xenophon's Kerasous lay at Giresun or further west is debated, and Bryer & Winfield discuss the options. The foundation date is inferred from Pharnaces' reign.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Pharnakeia (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.389, "certainty": "probable", "role": "new royal city"}, {"name": "Kotyora (Ordu)", "lat": 40.985, "lon": 37.879, "certainty": "probable", "role": "people moved to Pharnakeia"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "taken by Pharnaces I"}, {"name": "Pharnakeia / Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "new royal foundation"}]}, {"id": "H2-011", "date": "-183", "date_text": "183 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Pharnaces I takes Sinope", "summary": "Pharnaces took Sinope in 183: Strabo says the city 'was taken by siege and was subject first to Pharnaces' (12.3.11), and Rhodian envoys complained about it at Rome in the winter of 183/2 (Polybius 23.9.2). Sinope later became the royal city: Mithridates VI Eupator was born and raised there and, in Strabo's words, 'treated it as the metropolis of the kingdom'. That the court moved there already in 183 is a modern inference. The capture made Pontus a sea power with the best harbour of the southern coast.", "people": ["Pharnaces I of Pontus"], "sources_primary": ["Polybius 23.9.2", "Strabo 12.3.11", "Livy 40.2.6-8"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1", "Magie 1950, vol. 1"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Strabo credits Eupator, not Pharnaces, with making Sinope the 'metropolis' (12.3.11).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "captured; later royal capital"}]}, {"id": "H2-013", "date": "-183", "date_end": "-179", "date_text": "183-179 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "The Pontic War of Pharnaces", "summary": "Pharnaces, allied with Mithridates, ruler of Lesser Armenia, invaded Galatia and Paphlagonia and took Tios on the Bithynian border. Eumenes II of Pergamon, Ariarathes IV of Cappadocia and later Prusias II fought him, while Roman embassies came and went. Polybius is the near-contemporary source.", "people": ["Pharnaces I of Pontus", "Eumenes II", "Ariarathes IV", "Prusias II", "Mithridates of Lesser Armenia"], "sources_primary": ["Polybius 23.9; 24.1, 24.5, 24.14-15", "Livy 40.20.1", "Diodorus 29.22-24"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1", "Hansen 1971 (The Attalids)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The course of the war is known only in fragments.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Tios", "lat": 41.553, "lon": 32.026, "role": "taken by Pharnaces"}, {"name": "Paphlagonia (Gangra)", "lat": 40.6, "lon": 33.615, "role": "invaded"}, {"name": "Galatia (Ankyra)", "lat": 39.94, "lon": 32.86, "role": "invaded; Eumenes' campaigns"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "Pharnaces' base"}, {"name": "Cappadocia (Mazaka)", "lat": 38.72, "lon": 35.49, "role": "home of Ariarathes IV"}]}, {"id": "H2-014", "date": "-179", "date_text": "179 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Peace of 179: Pharnaces keeps Sinope", "summary": "Under the treaty quoted by Polybius, Pharnaces evacuated Galatia and Paphlagonia, gave back Tios, and paid 900 talents to Ariarathes and 300 to Eumenes; Mithridates of Armenia paid 300. Sinope stayed Pontic. The treaty names as parties or adherents Artaxias of Armenia, Sarmatian chiefs, and the free cities of Heraclea, Mesembria, Chersonesos and Cyzicus, which shows how far Pontic diplomacy reached.", "people": ["Pharnaces I of Pontus", "Eumenes II", "Ariarathes IV", "Artaxias I"], "sources_primary": ["Polybius 25.2"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "kept by Pontus"}, {"name": "Tios", "lat": 41.553, "lon": 32.026, "role": "returned"}, {"name": "Chersonesos", "lat": 44.611, "lon": 33.493, "role": "adherent city"}, {"name": "Heraclea Pontica", "lat": 41.283, "lon": 31.417, "role": "adherent city"}]}, {"id": "H2-015", "date": "-179", "date_text": "179 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Borders 179 BC: Pontus as a coastal power", "summary": "After the Pontic War the kingdom held interior Pontus (Amaseia, Gaziura, Komana, Zela, the Iris and Lykos valleys) and the coast from Amastris through Sinope and Amisos to Kotyora and Pharnakeia. Trapezous may have come under Pontic control in this period, perhaps through the ally in Lesser Armenia, but there is no direct evidence. Lesser Armenia and Colchis were outside the kingdom.", "people": ["Pharnaces I of Pontus"], "sources_primary": ["Polybius 25.2", "Strabo 12.3.11, 12.3.17"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The status of Amisos and Trapezous in 179 is uncertain.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amastris", "lat": 41.747, "lon": 32.386, "role": "inside (west coast end)"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "inside (capital)"}, {"name": "Amisos", "lat": 41.3, "lon": 36.34, "role": "inside (probable)"}, {"name": "Pharnakeia", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "inside (east coast end)"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.727, "role": "uncertain"}, {"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Zela", "lat": 40.303, "lon": 35.886, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Lesser Armenia", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 38.12, "role": "outside (allied dynast)"}]}, {"id": "H2-016", "date": "-179", "date_end": "-155", "date_text": "179 or 155 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Treaty of Pharnaces with Chersonesos", "summary": "An inscription from Chersonesos in the Crimea records an oath of alliance in which Pharnaces promised to protect the city's democracy and freedom against the Scythians. It is the first sign of the Pontic kings' role north of the sea that Mithridates VI later turned into an empire.", "people": ["Pharnaces I of Pontus"], "sources_primary": ["IOSPE I(2) 402"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1", "Saprykin 1997"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The date depends on which era the inscription's 'year 157' counts from: 179 BC or 155 BC are both defended.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Chersonesos (Sevastopol)", "lat": 44.611, "lon": 33.493, "role": "city of the inscription"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "Pharnaces' capital"}]}, {"id": "H2-017", "date": "-160", "date_text": "160/159 BC (or 196/195)", "precision": "year", "title": "Athens honours Pharnaces and Nysa", "summary": "An Athenian decree found on Delos honours King Pharnaces and Queen Nysa for gifts to Athens. It is early evidence of the dynasty's courting of Greek opinion, which Mithridates VI pursued on a much larger scale.", "people": ["Pharnaces I of Pontus", "Nysa"], "sources_primary": ["OGIS 771 (Inscr. Delos 1497bis)"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1", "Tracy (on the archon date)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The archon Tychandros is dated to 160/59 by most epigraphists; an earlier date (196/5) was once argued.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Delos", "lat": 37.4, "lon": 25.27, "role": "findspot of the decree"}, {"name": "Athens", "lat": 37.97, "lon": 23.73, "role": "honouring city"}]}, {"id": "H2-018", "date": "-155", "date_text": "C. 159/155-c. 150 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Mithridates IV Philopator Philadelphos and Laodice", "summary": "Pharnaces' brother Mithridates IV ruled with his sister-wife Laodice. In 154 he sent help to Attalus II of Pergamon against Prusias II of Bithynia, keeping Pontus on Rome's side.", "people": ["Mithridates IV of Pontus", "Laodice (wife of Mithridates IV)", "Attalus II", "Prusias II"], "sources_primary": ["Polybius 33.12.1", "Royal coins and dedications"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 1"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "His reign is dated only approximately.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "royal capital"}, {"name": "Pergamon", "lat": 39.132, "lon": 27.184, "role": "ally"}]}, {"id": "H2-019", "date": "-150", "date_end": "-120", "date_text": "C. 150-120 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Mithridates V Euergetes, friend of Rome", "summary": "Mithridates V sent ships to Rome in the Third Punic War (149-146) and fought for Rome against Aristonicus in the war over the Pergamene inheritance (133-129). In reward Manius Aquillius gave him Phrygia Major (129), which the Senate revoked after his death. He married his daughter Laodice to Ariarathes VI of Cappadocia and extended Pontic influence into Paphlagonia and Galatia.", "people": ["Mithridates V Euergetes", "Manius Aquillius (consul 129)", "Aristonicus", "Ariarathes VI"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 10, 12, 57", "Strabo 14.1.38", "Justin 37.1.2; 38.5.3", "Eutropius 4.20"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 2", "Sherwin-White 1984"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The Phrygian grant was later called a bribe by Roman sources; its exact extent is unclear.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "royal capital"}, {"name": "Phrygia Major (approximate centre)", "lat": 38.95, "lon": 30.5, "role": "granted 129, revoked c. 116"}, {"name": "Cappadocia (Mazaka)", "lat": 38.72, "lon": 35.49, "role": "dynastic marriage"}]}, {"id": "H2-020", "date": "-120", "date_text": "C. 120 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Mithridates V murdered at Sinope; accession of Mithridates VI", "summary": "Mithridates V was assassinated by members of his court at Sinope. His son Mithridates VI Eupator, born at Sinope c. 134/132, was still a boy; his mother Laodice ruled as regent with his younger brother. Justin's story of Eupator's years in hiding, hunting in the wild and taking antidotes, is legend built on his later fame; the comets said to mark his birth and accession may match comets recorded in Chinese annals for 135 and 119 BC.", "people": ["Mithridates V Euergetes", "Mithridates VI Eupator", "Laodice (mother of Eupator)", "Mithridates Chrestus"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 10.4.10; 12.3.11", "Justin 37.1.6-37.2.9", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 22.2"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 2", "Mayor 2010, chs. 3-5", "Ramsey 1999 (HSCP 99)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "He was born in 134 or 132 (Appian 112 gives his age at death as 68 or 69). The years in hiding are romance.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "place of the murder and of Eupator's birth"}]}, {"id": "H2-021", "date": "-120", "date_text": "C. 120 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Borders c. 120 BC: the inheritance of Eupator", "summary": "At Eupator's accession the kingdom held Pontus proper from Amastris to Pharnakeia and inland to Zela and the Lykos, plus influence (not rule) in Paphlagonia, Galatia and Cappadocia. Phrygia Major, granted in 129, was taken back by the Senate c. 116. Lesser Armenia (under Antipater son of Sisis), Colchis and the Bosporus were independent.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 12.3.1", "Justin 38.5.3"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 2"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amastris", "lat": 41.747, "lon": 32.386, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "inside (capital)"}, {"name": "Pharnakeia", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Zela", "lat": 40.303, "lon": 35.886, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Lesser Armenia", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 38.12, "role": "outside"}, {"name": "Colchis", "lat": 42.15, "lon": 41.67, "role": "outside"}, {"name": "Bosporan kingdom", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "outside"}]}, {"id": "H2-022", "date": "-115", "date_end": "-113", "date_text": "C. 116-113 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Eupator takes sole power", "summary": "Returning to Sinope, the young king imprisoned his mother and later had her and his brother killed, then married his sister Laodice. From this point he ruled alone and began a programme of expansion around the Black Sea.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Laodice (mother of Eupator)", "Mithridates Chrestus", "Laodice (sister-wife)"], "sources_primary": ["Memnon, FGrH 434 F 22.2", "Appian, Mith. 112", "Sallust, Histories fr. (Mithridates' letter)"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 2", "Mayor 2010"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The exact year is unknown (about 116–113).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "seat of the coup"}]}, {"id": "H2-023", "date": "-115", "date_end": "-106", "date_text": "C. 115-106 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Lesser Armenia ceded to Mithridates; 75 treasure-forts", "summary": "Antipater son of Sisis, ruler of Lesser Armenia, handed his country to Mithridates. Strabo says the king built 75 fortresses there, in which he kept most of his treasure; Pompey later destroyed many of them. Lesser Armenia linked Pontus to the upper Euphrates and to Armenia proper.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Antipater son of Sisis"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 12.3.28"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 3"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The date of the cession is not given; most place it before 106.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Lesser Armenia (later Nicopolis)", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 38.12, "role": "region annexed"}, {"name": "Upper Lykos / Satala area", "lat": 40.03, "lon": 39.6, "role": "eastern part of the region"}]}, {"id": "H2-024", "date": "-110", "date_text": "C. 115-105 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Colchis annexed", "summary": "Mithridates took Colchis, the rich coastland of the Phasis with its timber, flax, pitch and wax for shipbuilding, and ruled it through governors; Moaphernes, Strabo's great-uncle, held the post late in the reign. The Colchian coast connected Pontus by sea to the Bosporus.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Moaphernes"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 11.2.18; 12.3.33", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 22.4"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 3", "Braund 1994 (Georgia in Antiquity)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The date is unknown; it is sometimes put after the Crimean campaigns.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Phasis (Poti)", "lat": 42.15, "lon": 41.67, "role": "Greek port of Colchis"}, {"name": "Dioskourias (Sukhumi)", "lat": 42.995, "lon": 41.01, "role": "Greek port of Colchis"}]}, {"id": "H2-025", "date": "-110", "date_end": "-107", "date_text": "C. 110-107 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Diophantus' campaigns in the Crimea; the Bosporus becomes Pontic", "summary": "Chersonesos asked Mithridates for help against the Scythians of Palakos. His general Diophantus of Sinope fought three campaigns, defeated the Scythians, put down the revolt of Saumakos, who had killed King Paerisades V at Pantikapaion, and secured the Bosporan kingdom for Mithridates. A long decree of Chersonesos honouring Diophantus is the contemporary record.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Diophantus son of Asclepiodorus", "Palakos", "Saumakos", "Paerisades V"], "sources_primary": ["IOSPE I(2) 352 (Diophantus decree)", "Strabo 7.4.3, 7.4.4, 7.4.7"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 3", "Saprykin 1997", "Mayor 2010"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Absolute dates of the three campaigns (c. 110-107 or 112-109) and of Saumakos' revolt are debated.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Chersonesos", "lat": 44.611, "lon": 33.493, "role": "city that asked for help; findspot of the decree"}, {"name": "Neapolis Scythica (Simferopol)", "lat": 44.95, "lon": 34.13, "role": "Scythian royal seat attacked"}, {"name": "Theodosia", "lat": 45.03, "lon": 35.38, "role": "Bosporan city"}, {"name": "Pantikapaion (Kerch)", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "Bosporan capital; Saumakos' revolt"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "home of Diophantus; base of the fleet"}]}, {"id": "H2-026", "date": "-108", "date_text": "C. 108/107 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Paphlagonia and Galatia partitioned with Nicomedes III", "summary": "Mithridates and Nicomedes III of Bithynia shared out Paphlagonia, and Mithridates also occupied part of Galatia. When Roman envoys ordered them out, Mithridates answered that the land was his father's inheritance.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Nicomedes III of Bithynia"], "sources_primary": ["Justin 37.4.3-5; 38.5.4"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 3"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The date is approximate.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Paphlagonia (Gangra)", "lat": 40.6, "lon": 33.615, "role": "partitioned"}, {"name": "Inner Paphlagonia", "lat": 41.513, "lon": 34.215, "role": "partitioned"}]}, {"id": "H2-027", "date": "-107", "date_text": "C. 107 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Borders c. 107 BC: the Black Sea empire", "summary": "Before he moved against Rome, Mithridates' 'Pontic empire' comprised Pontus from Amastris to Trapezous, Lesser Armenia, Colchis to Dioskourias, the Bosporan kingdom on both sides of the Kerch strait, a protectorate over Chersonesos, and shares of Paphlagonia and Galatia. Alliances with Olbia, Tyras and the Scythians, Sarmatians and Bastarnae extended his reach round the north and west coasts.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 7.4.3-7; 11.2.18; 12.3.1; 12.3.28", "IOSPE I(2) 352", "Justin 37.4; 38.7"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 3", "Hojte 2009 (ed.)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The 'alliances' on the north and west coasts are loosely attested, and the borders in the steppe are notional.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amastris", "lat": 41.747, "lon": 32.386, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "inside (capital)"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.727, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Lesser Armenia", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 38.12, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Colchis (Phasis)", "lat": 42.15, "lon": 41.67, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Dioskourias", "lat": 42.995, "lon": 41.01, "role": "inside (north-east coast end)"}, {"name": "Pantikapaion", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "inside (Bosporus)"}, {"name": "Phanagoria", "lat": 45.28, "lon": 36.97, "role": "inside (Bosporus)"}, {"name": "Chersonesos", "lat": 44.611, "lon": 33.493, "role": "protectorate"}, {"name": "Olbia", "lat": 46.69, "lon": 31.9, "role": "allied city"}, {"name": "Paphlagonia", "lat": 40.6, "lon": 33.615, "role": "shared with Bithynia"}]}, {"id": "H2-028", "date": "-102", "date_text": "102/101 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "The monument of Helianax on Delos", "summary": "Helianax, priest of Poseidon and of the Kabeiroi, built on Delos a shrine for Mithridates VI and his court, with portrait medallions of the king's Greek courtiers (among them Dorylaus, his foster-brother, and Papias of Amisos, his chief physician) and of fellow kings. It shows a Pontic court presented to the Greek world as Greek.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Helianax", "Dorylaus", "Papias of Amisos"], "sources_primary": ["I.Délos 1572 = OGIS 372: statue of Dorylaos of Amisos, son of Philetairos, foster-brother of the king and commander of the bodyguard, dedicated by Helianax son of Asklepiodoros, priest, 102/1 BC (attalus.org/docs/ogis/s372.html)", "I.Délos 1581 = OGIS 430: statue of a 'first friend' of Arsakes (Mithridates II of Parthia), dedicated by Helianax, c. 102/1 BC (attalus.org/docs/ogis/s430.html)"], "sources_modern": ["Erciyas 2006", "McGing 1986, ch. 4", "Kreuz 2009 (in Hojte ed.)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The inscriptions of the monument are published in the Inscriptions de Délos, in a series that runs at least to no. 1581. I.Délos 1560, a dedication of statues of Mithridates and his brother by Dionysios son of Neon (about 115/4 BC), is a separate monument.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Delos", "lat": 37.4, "lon": 25.27, "role": "the monument"}]}, {"id": "H2-029", "date": "-101", "date_end": "-95", "date_text": "C. 101-95 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Cappadocia: Ariarathes IX and the Roman veto", "summary": "Mithridates had his brother-in-law Ariarathes VI murdered (c. 116), killed Ariarathes VII at a parley (c. 100) and set his own eight-year-old son on the throne as Ariarathes IX. The Senate ordered him out of Cappadocia and Paphlagonia; the Cappadocians chose Ariobarzanes I, whom Sulla installed (in 96 or 92).", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Ariarathes VI", "Ariarathes VII", "Ariarathes IX", "Ariobarzanes I", "Sulla"], "sources_primary": ["Justin 38.1-2", "Plutarch, Sulla 5", "Appian, Mith. 10", "Strabo 12.2.11"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 4", "Sherwin-White 1984"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Sulla's mission to Cappadocia is dated 96 or 92 BC.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Mazaka (Kayseri)", "lat": 38.72, "lon": 35.49, "role": "Cappadocian royal seat"}]}, {"id": "H2-030", "date": "-94", "date_text": "C. 94/93 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Alliance with Tigranes II of Armenia", "summary": "Mithridates married his daughter Cleopatra to Tigranes II, the new king of Armenia, and the two agreed that Mithridates would take cities and land and Tigranes the people and movable goods. The alliance made Armenia the refuge to which Mithridates fled in 71.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Tigranes II", "Cleopatra of Pontus"], "sources_primary": ["Justin 38.3.1-5", "Plutarch, Lucullus 22"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 4"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Artaxata", "lat": 39.88, "lon": 44.58, "role": "Armenian royal city"}]}, {"id": "H2-031", "date": "-89", "date_text": "89 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Nicomedes IV raids Pontus; war begins", "summary": "Pressed by the Roman legate Manius Aquillius, Nicomedes IV of Bithynia raided Pontic territory as far as Amastris. Mithridates' protests went unanswered and he went to war.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Nicomedes IV", "Manius Aquillius", "Pelopidas (Pontic envoy)"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 11-17", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 22.3-5"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 4", "Sherwin-White 1984"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Responsibility for the war: Appian blames Roman greed; modern views range from Roman provocation (Sherwin-White) to Mithridates' long preparation (McGing).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amastris", "lat": 41.747, "lon": 32.386, "role": "limit of the Bithynian raid"}, {"name": "Nicomedia", "lat": 40.77, "lon": 29.92, "role": "Bithynian capital"}]}, {"id": "H2-032", "date": "-89", "date_text": "89 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Battle of the Amnias", "summary": "Mithridates' generals Archelaus and Neoptolemus destroyed the army of Nicomedes IV on the plain of the Amnias river in Paphlagonia, near later Pompeiopolis. Appian gives the Pontic army as 250,000 foot, 40,000 horse, 300 decked ships and 100 two-banked ships (figures certainly inflated).", "people": ["Archelaus", "Neoptolemus", "Nicomedes IV"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 17-18", "Strabo 12.3.40", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 22.6"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 4"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Appian's figures for the armies are not credible as given.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amnias river (Gokirmak) near Pompeiopolis", "lat": 41.5, "lon": 34.1, "role": "battlefield (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H2-033", "date": "-89", "date_end": "-88", "date_text": "89-88 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Roman armies collapse; Aquillius executed at Pergamon", "summary": "After a Pontic victory near Protopachium (Mount Scorobas) the Roman commanders scattered. Mithridates overran Bithynia, Phrygia, Mysia and the province of Asia, where many cities welcomed him. Manius Aquillius was captured, paraded on an ass, and killed at Pergamon by molten gold poured down his throat, a punishment for Roman greed.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Manius Aquillius", "Lucius Cassius", "Quintus Oppius"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 19-21", "Athenaeus 5.213b (Posidonius)", "Velleius 2.18"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 4", "Mayor 2010"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Protopachium and Mount Scorobas have not been located.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Protopachium / Mt Scorobas (location unknown, approximate)", "lat": 41.2, "lon": 31.9, "role": "battle"}, {"name": "Pergamon", "lat": 39.132, "lon": 27.184, "role": "execution of Aquillius; Mithridates' headquarters"}]}, {"id": "H2-034", "date": "-88", "date_text": "88 BC (spring or summer)", "precision": "year", "title": "The Asian Vespers", "summary": "Mithridates sent secret letters to his satraps and to the cities of Asia ordering that on the thirtieth day all Romans and Italians, with their wives, children and Italian freedmen, be killed and left unburied; slaves who betrayed masters were freed, and debtors who killed creditors had half their debts cancelled. Appian describes killings at Ephesus, Pergamon, Adramyttion, Kaunos and Tralles, often in sanctuaries. Ancient totals run from 80,000 to 150,000.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 22-23", "Plutarch, Sulla 24.4 (150,000)", "Valerius Maximus 9.2.ext.3 (80,000: 'una epistola LXXX civium Romanorum … interemit')", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 1, 22.9 (80,000 'on that one day')", "Cicero, De imperio Cn. Pompei 7"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 5", "Sherwin-White 1984", "Kallet-Marx 1995", "Mayor 2010, ch. 8"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The numbers run from 80,000 (Memnon, Valerius Maximus) to 150,000 (Plutarch); many modern scholars, such as Brunt, think the true number far lower. Whether the local Greeks took part willingly, as Appian implies, or under orders is also debated.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Ephesus", "lat": 37.94, "lon": 27.34, "role": "killings at the Artemision"}, {"name": "Pergamon", "lat": 39.132, "lon": 27.184, "role": "killings at the Asklepieion"}, {"name": "Adramyttion", "lat": 39.53, "lon": 26.99, "role": "killings in the sea"}, {"name": "Kaunos", "lat": 36.827, "lon": 28.622, "role": "killings at the statue of Hestia"}, {"name": "Tralles", "lat": 37.86, "lon": 27.83, "role": "killings at the temple of Concord"}]}, {"id": "H2-035", "date": "-88", "date_text": "88 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Siege of Rhodes fails", "summary": "Rhodes, the main Greek holdout, beat off Mithridates' fleet and his siege engine (the sambuca). Magnesia by Sipylos and Stratonikeia also resisted.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 24-27"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 5"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Rhodes", "lat": 36.44, "lon": 28.22, "role": "besieged"}]}, {"id": "H2-036", "date": "-88", "date_text": "88 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Sack of Delos; Athens goes over to Mithridates", "summary": "A Pontic fleet under Menophanes and Archelaus sacked Delos, the great Italian trading centre; Appian says 20,000 people, mostly Italians, were killed on Delos and the islands. Athens, led by Athenion and then Aristion, joined Mithridates, who presented himself as liberator of the Greeks.", "people": ["Archelaus", "Menophanes", "Athenion", "Aristion"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 28", "Pausanias 3.23.3-5", "Athenaeus 5.211d-215b (Posidonius)"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 5", "Habicht 1997 (Athens from Alexander to Antony)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The figure of 20,000 is Appian's alone.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Delos", "lat": 37.4, "lon": 25.27, "role": "sacked"}, {"name": "Athens", "lat": 37.97, "lon": 23.73, "role": "allied with Mithridates"}]}, {"id": "H2-037", "date": "-88", "date_text": "88 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Borders 88 BC: the greatest extent", "summary": "At the height of his power, besides the Black Sea empire of c. 107, Mithridates held Bithynia, Paphlagonia, Cappadocia, Galatia, Phrygia, the Roman province of Asia (except Rhodes, Magnesia by Sipylos, Stratonikeia and some Lycian cities), many Aegean islands, and through Archelaus Athens, Boeotia and Euboea (87-86). The king moved his court to Pergamon.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 20-29", "Plutarch, Sulla 11"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 5"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Pergamon", "lat": 39.132, "lon": 27.184, "role": "royal residence 88-86"}, {"name": "Bithynia", "lat": 40.77, "lon": 29.92, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Cappadocia", "lat": 38.72, "lon": 35.49, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Asia", "lat": 37.94, "lon": 27.34, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Rhodes", "lat": 36.44, "lon": 28.22, "role": "outside (resisted)"}, {"name": "Athens", "lat": 37.97, "lon": 23.73, "role": "allied (87-86)"}, {"name": "Boeotia", "lat": 38.495, "lon": 22.84, "role": "occupied (87-86)"}]}, {"id": "H2-038", "date": "-87", "date_end": "-86", "date_text": "87-1 March 86 BC", "precision": "day", "title": "Sulla besieges Athens and Piraeus", "summary": "Sulla landed in Greece in 87 and besieged Athens and Archelaus in Piraeus. Athens fell on the Kalends of March 86 after famine; Piraeus was taken and burned.", "people": ["Sulla", "Archelaus", "Aristion"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 30-41", "Plutarch, Sulla 12-14"], "sources_modern": ["Keaveney 1982/2005 (Sulla)", "Habicht 1997"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Athens", "lat": 37.97, "lon": 23.73, "role": "taken 1 March 86"}, {"name": "Piraeus", "lat": 37.94, "lon": 23.64, "role": "besieged and destroyed"}]}, {"id": "H2-039", "date": "-86", "date_text": "86 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Battle of Chaeronea", "summary": "Sulla routed the Pontic army of Archelaus and Taxiles in Boeotia. The Pontic army is given as 110,000–120,000: Plutarch has Taxiles bring 100,000 foot, 10,000 horse and 90 scythed chariots (Sulla 15.1) but speaks of 120,000 later (22.4), and Appian gives 120,000 (Mith. 41); the Romans were fewer than 17,000. Sulla's memoirs claimed only 14 men missing, of whom two came back (Plutarch, Sulla 19.4); Appian has 15, two of whom returned (45). Only about 10,000 of the Pontic army escaped (Plutarch; Appian).", "people": ["Sulla", "Archelaus", "Taxiles"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 41–45", "Plutarch, Sulla 15–19 (15.1, 19.4), 22.4"], "sources_modern": ["Keaveney 2005", "McGing 1986, ch. 5"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "All the figures come from the winners' side, through Sulla's memoirs; the Pontic army is best given as a range, 110,000–120,000.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Chaeronea", "lat": 38.495, "lon": 22.84, "role": "battlefield"}]}, {"id": "H2-040", "date": "-85", "date_text": "Late 86 or 85 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Battle of Orchomenos", "summary": "A second Pontic army under Dorylaus and Archelaus was destroyed by Sulla in the marshes at Orchomenos. Pontic power in Greece ended.", "people": ["Sulla", "Archelaus", "Dorylaus"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 49-50", "Plutarch, Sulla 20-21"], "sources_modern": ["Keaveney 2005"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The battle is dated late 86 or early 85.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Orchomenos", "lat": 38.49, "lon": 22.975, "role": "battlefield"}]}, {"id": "H2-041", "date": "-85", "date_text": "86-85 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Fimbria's campaign in Asia", "summary": "Fimbria murdered the consul Flaccus at Nicomedia, took his army, defeated the king's son at the Rhyndakos, nearly trapped Mithridates at Pitane (Lucullus' fleet refused to help) and sacked Ilion.", "people": ["Fimbria", "Lucius Valerius Flaccus", "Mithridates VI Eupator", "Lucullus"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 51-53", "Plutarch, Lucullus 3", "Plutarch, Sulla 23", "Orosius 6.2.11"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 5"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Nicomedia", "lat": 40.77, "lon": 29.92, "role": "waypoint 1: Flaccus killed"}, {"name": "Miletopolis / Rhyndakos", "lat": 40.1, "lon": 28.43, "role": "waypoint 2: victory over the king's son (approximate)"}, {"name": "Pergamon", "lat": 39.132, "lon": 27.184, "role": "waypoint 3: taken"}, {"name": "Pitane", "lat": 38.93, "lon": 26.94, "role": "waypoint 4: Mithridates escapes by sea"}, {"name": "Ilion", "lat": 39.957, "lon": 26.239, "role": "waypoint 5: sacked"}]}, {"id": "H2-042", "date": "-85", "date_text": "Autumn 85 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Peace of Dardanus", "summary": "Sulla and Mithridates met at Dardanos in the Troad. Mithridates gave up all his conquests (Asia, Bithynia, Paphlagonia, Cappadocia) and paid an indemnity of 2,000 talents and 70 bronze-armoured ships with their equipment (Plutarch, Sulla 22.5; 70 ships and 500 archers at the handover, 24.3); Memnon gives 80 triremes and 3,000 talents, paid to Sulla personally (F 1, 25.2). He kept his kingdom and was recognised as a friend of Rome.", "people": ["Sulla", "Mithridates VI Eupator"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 54-58", "Plutarch, Sulla 22-24 (22.5, 24.3)", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 1, 25.2"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 5", "Keaveney 2005"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The indemnity and fleet are given as 2,000 talents and 70 ships (Plutarch) or 3,000 talents and 80 triremes (Memnon).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Dardanos", "lat": 40.08, "lon": 26.36, "role": "meeting place"}]}, {"id": "H2-043", "date": "-85", "date_text": "85 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Borders 85 BC: back to the Black Sea", "summary": "After Dardanus Mithridates kept Pontus, Lesser Armenia and Colchis. The Bosporan cities and Colchis rose against him in the following years; he recovered Colchis, and the Bosporus was retaken c. 80 under his son Machares.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Machares"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 58, 64, 67"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 6"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "inside (capital)"}, {"name": "Lesser Armenia", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 38.12, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Colchis", "lat": 42.15, "lon": 41.67, "role": "in revolt, then recovered"}, {"name": "Bosporus", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "in revolt until c. 80"}, {"name": "Bithynia", "lat": 40.77, "lon": 29.92, "role": "outside (restored to Nicomedes IV)"}, {"name": "Cappadocia", "lat": 38.72, "lon": 35.49, "role": "outside (Ariobarzanes I)"}]}, {"id": "H2-044", "date": "-83", "date_text": "83 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Second Mithridatic War: Murena invades", "summary": "Sulla's legate Lucius Licinius Murena, on a pretext, invaded Pontic territory, attacked the rich temple-village of Komana and raided some 400 villages.", "people": ["Lucius Licinius Murena", "Mithridates VI Eupator", "Archelaus (defected to Rome)"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 64-65", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 26"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 6"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Which Komana Appian means is not certain.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Komana (probably Pontica)", "lat": 40.355, "lon": 36.635, "role": "attacked"}, {"name": "Cappadocia", "lat": 38.72, "lon": 35.49, "role": "Murena's route"}]}, {"id": "H2-045", "date": "-82", "date_text": "82 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Murena defeated on the Halys", "summary": "Mithridates beat Murena in a battle near the Halys. Sulla sent Aulus Gabinius to stop the war; Mithridates betrothed a daughter to Ariobarzanes and kept a slice of Cappadocia for a time (81).", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Lucius Licinius Murena", "Aulus Gabinius"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 65-66"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 6"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The site of the battle is unknown.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Halys river (battle site unknown, approximate)", "lat": 40.1, "lon": 34.9, "role": "battle"}]}, {"id": "H2-046", "date": "-80", "date_text": "C. 80 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Machares king of the Bosporus; disaster among the Achaeans", "summary": "Mithridates retook the Bosporus and made his son Machares king there. A campaign against the 'Achaeans', a people of the Caucasus coast north of Colchis, cost him two thirds of his army.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Machares"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 67, 102"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 6"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Pantikapaion", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "Machares' seat"}, {"name": "Achaean coast (Tuapse area, approximate)", "lat": 44.1, "lon": 39.07, "role": "failed campaign"}]}, {"id": "H2-047", "date": "-74", "date_text": "Late 75 or early 74 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Bithynia bequeathed to Rome; alliance with Sertorius", "summary": "Nicomedes IV died and left Bithynia to Rome, which put Roman power on Pontus' western border. Mithridates had already made an alliance with the Roman rebel Sertorius in Spain (c. 75), who sent him officers.", "people": ["Nicomedes IV", "Mithridates VI Eupator", "Quintus Sertorius", "Marcus Marius"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 68-71", "Plutarch, Sertorius 23-24", "Livy, Per. 93", "Eutropius 6.6"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 7", "Sherwin-White 1984"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The date of Nicomedes' death, 75 or 74, is tied to the debate over when the Third War began.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Nicomedia", "lat": 40.77, "lon": 29.92, "role": "Bithynian capital"}]}, {"id": "H2-048", "date": "-73", "date_text": "73 BC (or 74)", "precision": "year", "title": "Third Mithridatic War: invasion of Bithynia; Cotta beaten at Chalcedon", "summary": "Mithridates invaded Bithynia with a great army and fleet and defeated the consul Marcus Aurelius Cotta by land and sea at Chalcedon.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Marcus Aurelius Cotta", "Lucius Licinius Lucullus"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 69-71", "Plutarch, Lucullus 7-8", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 27"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 7", "Keaveney 1992 (Lucullus)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The war is dated to 74 (the older view) or 73 (McGing, Sherwin-White).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Chalcedon", "lat": 40.99, "lon": 29.03, "role": "battle"}]}, {"id": "H2-049", "date": "-73", "date_end": "-72", "date_text": "73-72 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Siege of Cyzicus", "summary": "Mithridates besieged Cyzicus by land and sea while Lucullus cut his supply lines. Hunger and plague broke the besiegers; Lucullus destroyed the retreating army at the Rhyndakos and at the Aisepos and Granikos.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Lucius Licinius Lucullus"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 72-76", "Plutarch, Lucullus 9-11", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 28", "Strabo 12.8.11"], "sources_modern": ["Keaveney 1992", "McGing 1986, ch. 7"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Plutarch's 300,000 for the Pontic army is an ancient figure, not a count.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Cyzicus", "lat": 40.385, "lon": 27.885, "role": "besieged"}, {"name": "Rhyndakos river", "lat": 40.17, "lon": 28.68, "role": "Pontic rout (approximate)"}, {"name": "Granikos / Aisepos", "lat": 40.2, "lon": 27.3, "role": "Pontic rout (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H2-050", "date": "-72", "date_text": "72 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Lucullus invades Pontus: Amisos, Eupatoria and Themiskyra besieged", "summary": "Lucullus marched through Galatia into Pontus and besieged Amisos, the neighbouring royal foundation Eupatoria, and Themiskyra on the Thermodon, whose defenders, Appian says, sent bears and swarms of bees into the Roman tunnels.", "people": ["Lucius Licinius Lucullus", "Mithridates VI Eupator"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 78", "Plutarch, Lucullus 14"], "sources_modern": ["Keaveney 1992", "McGing 1986, ch. 7"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Appian places Eupatoria next to Amisos; Strabo (12.3.30) places a half-built Eupatoria where the Lykos joins the Iris, later Pompey's Magnopolis. Whether these are two places or one is debated.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amisos", "lat": 41.3, "lon": 36.34, "role": "besieged"}, {"name": "Themiskyra (Terme)", "lat": 41.208, "lon": 36.972, "role": "besieged"}, {"name": "Eupatoria (by Amisos)", "lat": 41.3, "lon": 36.34, "role": "royal suburb/foundation besieged; exact site unknown"}]}, {"id": "H2-051", "date": "-71", "date_text": "Winter 72/71 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Battle of Kabeira; Mithridates flees to Armenia", "summary": "Mithridates wintered at Kabeira, his favourite residence. After cavalry battles and a failed supply war, panic broke out in his camp as he began to withdraw; the army dissolved and the king escaped by way of Komana to Tigranes in Armenia, who kept him waiting twenty months.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Lucius Licinius Lucullus", "Tigranes II"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 79-82", "Plutarch, Lucullus 15-19, 22", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 29"], "sources_modern": ["Keaveney 1992", "McGing 1986, ch. 7"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Winter 72/71 is the usual date; some put the battle in 72.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kabeira (Niksar)", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.955, "role": "battle and royal residence"}, {"name": "Komana Pontica", "lat": 40.355, "lon": 36.635, "role": "waypoint 1 of the flight"}, {"name": "Lesser Armenia", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 38.12, "role": "waypoint 2 of the flight"}, {"name": "Armenia (Artaxata)", "lat": 39.88, "lon": 44.58, "role": "waypoint 3: refuge with Tigranes"}]}, {"id": "H2-052", "date": "-71", "date_text": "71 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "The royal women killed at Pharnakeia", "summary": "Fearing they would fall into Roman hands, Mithridates sent the eunuch Bacchides to kill his sisters Roxane and Statira and his wives Berenice of Chios and Monime of Miletus, who were kept at Pharnakeia. Plutarch's account of Monime's death became famous; it also shows how many of the king's wives were Greeks of the Aegean cities.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Monime of Miletus", "Berenice of Chios", "Roxane", "Statira", "Bacchides"], "sources_primary": ["Plutarch, Lucullus 18"], "sources_modern": ["Mayor 2010, ch. 13", "McGing 1986"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Plutarch is the only source.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Pharnakeia (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "place of the killings"}]}, {"id": "H2-053", "date": "-71", "date_text": "71 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Fall of Amisos", "summary": "Amisos was taken after a long siege; its commander, the engineer Callimachus, set fire to the city as he escaped by sea. Lucullus, recalling Sulla at Athens, wept, rebuilt the city, freed it and resettled its people; among the captives was the grammarian Tyrannion of Amisos, later Strabo's teacher in Rome.", "people": ["Lucius Licinius Lucullus", "Callimachus (engineer)", "Tyrannion of Amisos"], "sources_primary": ["Plutarch, Lucullus 19", "Appian, Mith. 83", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 30", "Strabo 12.3.16"], "sources_modern": ["Keaveney 1992"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Dated 71, by some 70.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amisos (Samsun)", "lat": 41.3, "lon": 36.34, "role": "taken and burned; rebuilt by Lucullus"}]}, {"id": "H2-054", "date": "-70", "date_text": "70 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Fall of Sinope", "summary": "Sinope, held by a Cilician garrison under Leonippus and Cleochares (Strabo names Bacchides, who oppressed the citizens), was stormed by Lucullus; the garrison burned the heavier ships and fled. Lucullus freed the city but took away the statue of its founder Autolykos and the sphere of Billaros.", "people": ["Lucius Licinius Lucullus", "Leonippus", "Cleochares", "Bacchides"], "sources_primary": ["Plutarch, Lucullus 23", "Strabo 12.3.11", "Appian, Mith. 83", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 37", "Orosius 6.3.2"], "sources_modern": ["Keaveney 1992"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "taken"}]}, {"id": "H2-055", "date": "-70", "date_text": "70 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Amaseia surrenders; Strabo's family changes sides", "summary": "Amaseia and the remaining Pontic fortresses surrendered. Strabo records that his mother's father, angered by the king's execution of his cousin Tibius and Tibius' son Theophilus, made terms with Lucullus and brought fifteen garrisons over to him. Machares in the Bosporus also made peace with Lucullus.", "people": ["Lucius Licinius Lucullus", "Machares", "Strabo's maternal grandfather", "Tibius", "Theophilus"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 83", "Strabo 12.3.33; 10.4.10", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 37"], "sources_modern": ["Dueck 2000, ch. 1", "Keaveney 1992"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "surrendered"}, {"name": "Pantikapaion", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "Machares goes over to Rome"}]}, {"id": "H2-056", "date": "-70", "date_text": "70 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Borders 70 BC: Pontus under Lucullus", "summary": "Under the Roman occupation, Roman forces held the Pontic coast (Amastris, Sinope, Amisos, Pharnakeia) and the interior (Amaseia, Kabeira, Komana); Mithridates held nothing in Pontus, and the Bosporus under Machares was allied to Rome.", "people": ["Lucius Licinius Lucullus"], "sources_primary": ["Plutarch, Lucullus 19-23", "Appian, Mith. 82-83"], "sources_modern": ["Keaveney 1992"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "Roman-held"}, {"name": "Amisos", "lat": 41.3, "lon": 36.34, "role": "Roman-held"}, {"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "Roman-held"}, {"name": "Kabeira", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.955, "role": "Roman-held"}, {"name": "Bosporus", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "allied to Rome (Machares)"}]}, {"id": "H2-057", "date": "-69", "date_text": "6 October 69 BC", "precision": "day", "title": "Battle of Tigranocerta", "summary": "Lucullus invaded Armenia and defeated Tigranes' great army before his new capital, Tigranocerta, on the day before the Nones of October; Plutarch says Tigranes had over 250,000 men and Lucullus about 11,000, and that only five Romans died. The city, peopled by Greeks and others deported from Cappadocia and Cilicia, fell and Lucullus sent the deportees home.", "people": ["Lucius Licinius Lucullus", "Tigranes II", "Mithridates VI Eupator"], "sources_primary": ["Plutarch, Lucullus 24-29", "Appian, Mith. 84-86", "Memnon, FGrH 434 F 38", "Strabo 11.14.15; 12.2.9"], "sources_modern": ["Keaveney 1992", "Sinclair 1994-1997 (on the site)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The site of Tigranocerta is unresolved (Silvan, Arzan, or the Garzan valley proposed by Sinclair). Plutarch's numbers are Roman propaganda.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Melitene", "lat": 38.42, "lon": 38.36, "role": "waypoint 1 (Euphrates crossing area)"}, {"name": "Tigranocerta (proposed: near Silvan; alternatives Arzan, Garzan valley)", "lat": 38.14, "lon": 41.0, "role": "waypoint 2: battle and capture"}]}, {"id": "H2-058", "date": "-68", "date_end": "-67", "date_text": "68-67 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Artaxata and Nisibis; Lucullus' army balks", "summary": "Lucullus beat the allied kings again near Artaxata, but his troops refused to go on into the Armenian highlands; he turned south and took Nisibis in winter 68/67. Mutiny, stirred up by Publius Clodius, ended his offensive.", "people": ["Lucius Licinius Lucullus", "Tigranes II", "Mithridates VI Eupator", "Publius Clodius"], "sources_primary": ["Plutarch, Lucullus 31-34", "Cassius Dio 36.6-7, 36.14-17"], "sources_modern": ["Keaveney 1992"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Artaxata", "lat": 39.88, "lon": 44.58, "role": "battle nearby (68)"}, {"name": "Nisibis", "lat": 37.07, "lon": 41.21, "role": "taken (winter 68/67)"}]}, {"id": "H2-059", "date": "-67", "date_text": "67 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Mithridates returns: the Roman disaster at Zela", "summary": "Mithridates came back into Pontus, defeated the legate Fabius and then Triarius at Zela, where 7,000 Romans fell, among them 24 tribunes and 150 centurions. He regained most of his kingdom.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Gaius Valerius Triarius", "Marcus Fabius Hadrianus"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 88-89", "Plutarch, Lucullus 35", "Cassius Dio 36.12-13"], "sources_modern": ["Keaveney 1992", "McGing 1986, ch. 7"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Zela (Zile)", "lat": 40.303, "lon": 35.886, "role": "battle"}]}, {"id": "H2-060", "date": "-66", "date_text": "Summer 66 BC", "precision": "month", "title": "Pompey's night victory near Nicopolis", "summary": "Given the command by the lex Manilia, Pompey trapped Mithridates in Lesser Armenia and attacked his camp at night by the light of a low moon, which threw the Romans' shadows far ahead of them. Plutarch says 'many more than ten thousand' Pontic soldiers fell (Pomp. 32.7), Orosius 40,000 killed or captured (6.4.5); Mithridates broke out with 800 horsemen and fled with three companions (Pomp. 32.8), among them his concubine Hypsikrateia, who rode and fought dressed as a man. Pompey founded Nicopolis, 'Victory City', on the site.", "people": ["Pompey", "Mithridates VI Eupator", "Hypsikrateia"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 97-101", "Plutarch, Pompey 32 (32.7–8)", "Cassius Dio 36.45-50", "Strabo 12.3.28", "Livy, Per. 101", "Orosius 6.4.3-5 (6.4.5: 'XL milia caesa uel capta')"], "sources_modern": ["Seager 2002 (Pompey)", "McGing 1986, ch. 7", "Mayor 2010, ch. 15", "Bernard 2006, CRAI, pp. 280–288 (the Phanagoria epitaph)", "Gabelko 2013 (on the epitaph)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Pontic losses run from 'many more than ten thousand slain' (Plutarch) to 40,000 killed or captured (Orosius, who gives the Roman losses as 1,000 wounded and scarcely 40 killed). The site of the battle (Dasteira?) is debated. An epitaph found at Phanagoria in 2004 naming 'Hypsikrates, wife of King Mithridates Eupator Dionysos' (Bernard 2006; Heinen 2012; Gabelko 2013) appears to confirm Hypsikrateia.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Nicopolis (Purk, near Susehri)", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 38.12, "role": "battle site and later city"}, {"name": "Sinora (on the Armenian border; location unknown, approximate)", "lat": 40.15, "lon": 39.3, "role": "Mithridates' treasure-fort, first refuge"}]}, {"id": "H2-061", "date": "-66", "date_end": "-65", "date_text": "Winter 66/65-65 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Mithridates' flight round the Caucasus to the Bosporus", "summary": "Mithridates wintered at Dioskourias in Colchis, then marched along the Caucasus coast through the lands of the Heniochoi, Zygoi and Achaeans to the Bosporus, where his son Machares killed himself (or was killed). He planned, according to Appian, to march round the Black Sea and up the Danube to invade Italy.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Machares"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 101-102, 109", "Cassius Dio 36.50; 37.3", "Plutarch, Pompey 32.9-11"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 7", "Mayor 2010"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The route over the coastal mountains is not recorded; only the peoples along the coast are named.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Nicopolis battlefield", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 38.12, "role": "waypoint 1"}, {"name": "Phasis", "lat": 42.15, "lon": 41.67, "role": "waypoint 2"}, {"name": "Dioskourias", "lat": 42.995, "lon": 41.01, "role": "waypoint 3: winter quarters 66/65"}, {"name": "Heniochoi coast (Pityous)", "lat": 43.16, "lon": 40.34, "role": "waypoint 4"}, {"name": "Achaean coast (approximate)", "lat": 44.1, "lon": 39.07, "role": "waypoint 5"}, {"name": "Gorgippia (Anapa), Sindike", "lat": 44.89, "lon": 37.32, "role": "waypoint 6"}, {"name": "Pantikapaion", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "waypoint 7: arrival"}]}, {"id": "H2-062", "date": "-65", "date_text": "65 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Pompey in the Caucasus", "summary": "Pompey followed into Iberia and Albania (eastern Georgia and Azerbaijan), fought the Albanians near the Kyros (Kura) and reached the Phasis, where his fleet under Servilius blockaded the Pontic coast. He then turned back, judging Mithridates out of reach.", "people": ["Pompey", "Artoces of Iberia", "Oroeses of Albania", "Servilius"], "sources_primary": ["Plutarch, Pompey 34-36", "Appian, Mith. 103", "Cassius Dio 37.1-5"], "sources_modern": ["Seager 2002", "Braund 1994"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Iberia (Mtskheta-Tbilisi area)", "lat": 41.7, "lon": 44.8, "role": "campaign"}, {"name": "Albania (Kura valley, approximate)", "lat": 41.2, "lon": 46.9, "role": "battle on the Kyros"}, {"name": "Phasis", "lat": 42.15, "lon": 41.67, "role": "furthest point; fleet base"}]}, {"id": "H2-063", "date": "-65", "date_end": "-64", "date_text": "65/64 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "The Heptakometai's mad honey", "summary": "Strabo tells that the Heptakometai, a mountain people between Trapezous and Pharnakeia (the Mossynoikoi of Xenophon), destroyed three of Pompey's maniples by setting out bowls of the 'maddening' rhododendron honey along the road and attacking the stupefied soldiers. It echoes Xenophon's account of the mad honey near Trapezous.", "people": ["Pompey", "Heptakometai"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 12.3.18", "cf. Xenophon, Anabasis 4.8.20-21"], "sources_modern": ["Mayor 2003 (Greek Fire, Poison Arrows and Scorpion Bombs)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Strabo gives no date; it is inferred from Pompey's movements.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Heptakometai country (mountains south of the coast between Pharnakeia and Trapezous, approximate)", "lat": 40.85, "lon": 38.9, "role": "ambush"}]}, {"id": "H2-064", "date": "-64", "date_end": "-63", "date_text": "64-63 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Pompey's settlement: Pontus et Bithynia and the new cities", "summary": "Pompey joined the western half of Pontus to Bithynia as a single province and divided it into eleven city territories (politeiai). He founded or refounded Nicopolis, Magnopolis (Eupatoria), Diospolis (Kabeira), Neapolis (Phazemon), Megalopolis (later Sebasteia), Pompeiopolis and Zela as cities. The east, with Lesser Armenia and the coast towards Colchis, went to allied kings (Deiotarus); the priesthood of Komana to Archelaus; inner Paphlagonia to the house of Pylaemenes.", "people": ["Pompey", "Deiotarus", "Archelaus (priest of Komana)"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 12.3.1, 12.3.28, 12.3.30-31, 12.3.37-38, 12.3.40", "Appian, Mith. 114-115", "Cassius Dio 37.20.2", "Pliny, Ep. 10.79 (lex Pompeia)", "Plutarch, Pompey 38"], "sources_modern": ["Marek 1993", "Madsen 2009 (Eager to be Roman)", "Mitchell 1993, vol. 1", "Magie 1950"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Which eleven cities formed the city territories is reconstructed differently (Marek; Madsen), and so is the eastern border of the province (Amisos, or the Iris).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Nicopolis", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 38.12, "role": "new city"}, {"name": "Magnopolis / Eupatoria (Lykos-Iris confluence, approximate)", "lat": 40.77, "lon": 36.48, "role": "new city"}, {"name": "Diospolis (Kabeira)", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.955, "role": "new city"}, {"name": "Neapolis (Phazemon, Vezirkopru)", "lat": 41.143, "lon": 35.455, "role": "new city"}, {"name": "Megalopolis (Sebasteia, Sivas)", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "new city"}, {"name": "Pompeiopolis (Taskopru)", "lat": 41.513, "lon": 34.215, "role": "new city"}, {"name": "Zela", "lat": 40.303, "lon": 35.886, "role": "made a city"}, {"name": "Komana", "lat": 40.355, "lon": 36.635, "role": "temple-state given to Archelaus"}, {"name": "Amisos", "lat": 41.3, "lon": 36.34, "role": "Pompey's winter quarters 65/64"}]}, {"id": "H2-065", "date": "-63", "date_text": "63 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Revolt of Phanagoria; death of Mithridates at Pantikapaion", "summary": "Exhausted by levies, Phanagoria revolted; Mithridates' son Pharnaces won over the army, which proclaimed him king. Mithridates, immune to the poison he took, had his Gallic officer Bituitus kill him in the citadel of Pantikapaion. He was 68 or 69 and had reigned 57 years. Pompey heard the news near Jericho.", "people": ["Mithridates VI Eupator", "Pharnaces II", "Bituitus", "Pompey"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 107-112", "Cassius Dio 37.10-14", "Plutarch, Pompey 41-42", "Orosius 6.5"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986, ch. 7", "Mayor 2010, ch. 16"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The season, spring or summer 63, is uncertain. The poison story is ancient tradition.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Phanagoria", "lat": 45.28, "lon": 36.97, "role": "revolt"}, {"name": "Pantikapaion (Mount Mithridates, Kerch)", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "death of the king"}]}, {"id": "H2-066", "date": "-63", "date_text": "63 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Mithridates buried at Sinope among the kings", "summary": "Pharnaces sent the embalmed body to Pompey, who gave it a royal funeral and had it buried in the tombs of the kings at Sinope. Pharnaces received the Bosporan kingdom, except Phanagoria, which was declared free.", "people": ["Pompey", "Pharnaces II", "Mithridates VI Eupator"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Mith. 113", "Plutarch, Pompey 42.4", "Cassius Dio 37.14"], "sources_modern": ["McGing 1986"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The Sinope tombs have not been found.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "royal tombs (not located)"}, {"name": "Bosporus", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "given to Pharnaces II"}]}, {"id": "H2-067", "date": "-63", "date_text": "63 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Borders 63 BC: Pompey's Pontus", "summary": "Pompey's settlement. The province of Pontus et Bithynia: Bithynia plus coastal and inner Pontus from Heraclea and Amastris through Sinope and Amisos, with interior cities such as Pompeiopolis, Neapolis, Amaseia, Diospolis, Magnopolis, Zela and Megalopolis. Client rulers: Deiotarus (Lesser Armenia, Gazelonitis and the coast east towards Trapezous), Archelaus (Komana), Attalus and the Pylaemenids (inner Paphlagonia), Aristarchus (Colchis), Pharnaces II (Bosporus, minus free Phanagoria).", "people": ["Pompey"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 12.3.1, 12.3.13, 12.3.38-39", "Appian, Mith. 114", "Cassius Dio 37.20"], "sources_modern": ["Marek 1993", "Sullivan 1980 (ANRW II.7.2)", "Mitchell 1993"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The eastern boundary of the province and the attribution of Amaseia, Trapezous and Pharnakeia are uncertain.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Heraclea", "lat": 41.283, "lon": 31.417, "role": "province (Pontic part begins)"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "province"}, {"name": "Amisos", "lat": 41.3, "lon": 36.34, "role": "province"}, {"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "province (probable)"}, {"name": "Zela", "lat": 40.303, "lon": 35.886, "role": "province"}, {"name": "Lesser Armenia", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 38.12, "role": "Deiotarus"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.727, "role": "client territory (Deiotarus?)"}, {"name": "Komana", "lat": 40.355, "lon": 36.635, "role": "Archelaus"}, {"name": "Inner Paphlagonia", "lat": 40.6, "lon": 33.615, "role": "Pylaemenid dynasts"}, {"name": "Colchis", "lat": 42.15, "lon": 41.67, "role": "Aristarchus"}, {"name": "Bosporus", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "Pharnaces II"}, {"name": "Phanagoria", "lat": 45.28, "lon": 36.97, "role": "free city"}]}, {"id": "H2-068", "date": "-61", "date_text": "28-29 September 61 BC", "precision": "day", "title": "Pompey's triumph over Mithridates", "summary": "Pompey's two-day triumph in Rome displayed the conquered peoples (Pontus, Armenia, Cappadocia, Paphlagonia, Media, Colchis, Iberia, Albania, the Heniochoi, Achaeans and others), Mithridates' treasures and his children. Pompey's dedication in the temple of Minerva, quoted by Pliny (NH 7.97), claimed 12,183,000 people 'routed, put to flight, killed or received in surrender', 846 ships sunk or taken and 1,538 towns and forts taken under Roman protection; his inscriptions also claimed 39 cities founded (Plutarch, Pomp. 45.2).", "people": ["Pompey"], "sources_primary": ["Plutarch, Pompey 45 (45.2: 39 cities)", "Appian, Mith. 116-117", "Pliny, NH 7.97 (the dedication: 12,183,000 people), 7.98 (the triumph, a.d. III Kal. Oct.); 37.12-16"], "sources_modern": ["Beard 2007 (The Roman Triumph)", "Seager 2002"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The figures are Pompey's own propaganda. Pliny's 12,183,000 counts people routed, killed or taken in surrender, not a population 'subdued'.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Rome", "lat": 41.89, "lon": 12.49, "role": "triumph"}]}, {"id": "H2-069", "date": "-48", "date_text": "December 48 BC", "precision": "month", "title": "Pharnaces II invades Pontus; victory at Nicopolis", "summary": "During Rome's civil war Pharnaces crossed from the Bosporus to recover his father's kingdom, overran Lesser Armenia, Cappadocia and Pontus, and defeated Caesar's legate Gnaeus Domitius Calvinus near Nicopolis.", "people": ["Pharnaces II", "Gnaeus Domitius Calvinus", "Deiotarus"], "sources_primary": ["Bellum Alexandrinum 34-40", "Cassius Dio 42.45-46", "Appian, Mith. 120"], "sources_modern": ["Sullivan 1990 (Near Eastern Royalty and Rome)"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Nicopolis", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 38.12, "role": "battle"}, {"name": "Pantikapaion", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "starting point"}]}, {"id": "H2-070", "date": "-48", "date_end": "-47", "date_text": "48/47 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Pharnaces takes Sinope and sacks Amisos", "summary": "Pharnaces captured Sinope and stormed Amisos, which had held to Rome; he killed or sold the citizens and castrated the boys.", "people": ["Pharnaces II"], "sources_primary": ["Bellum Alexandrinum 41", "Cassius Dio 42.46.3-4", "Appian, Mith. 120"], "sources_modern": ["Sullivan 1990"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amisos", "lat": 41.3, "lon": 36.34, "role": "sacked"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "taken"}]}, {"id": "H2-071", "date": "-47", "date_text": "2 August 47 BC", "precision": "day", "title": "Battle of Zela: 'veni, vidi, vici'", "summary": "Caesar, arriving from Egypt and Syria, met Pharnaces at Zela, where Pharnaces camped on the hill of his father's victory over Triarius. Pharnaces attacked uphill and was routed within about four hours. Caesar summed it up as 'I came, I saw, I conquered', in a letter (Plutarch) or on a placard in his Pontic triumph of 46 (Suetonius).", "people": ["Julius Caesar", "Pharnaces II"], "sources_primary": ["Bellum Alexandrinum 72-76", "Plutarch, Caesar 50", "Suetonius, Divus Julius 35.2, 37.2", "Appian, Civil Wars 2.91", "Cassius Dio 42.47-48", "Fasti Amiternini (date)"], "sources_modern": ["Gelzer 1968 (Caesar)", "Sullivan 1990", "Lord, L. E. 1938, 'The date of Julius Caesar's departure from Alexandria', JRS 28: 19–40 (Pharnaces 'defeated at Zela, in Pontus, on August 2nd', after the Roman calendars, CIL I, pp. 304 and 324)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The date is in the pre-Julian calendar: 2 August 47 is about 21 May 47 BC in the Julian calendar (J. Lendering, livius.org); Lord (1938) gives 2 August from the Roman calendars.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Zela (Zile)", "lat": 40.303, "lon": 35.886, "role": "battlefield"}]}, {"id": "H2-072", "date": "-47", "date_text": "47 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Caesar's settlement; Pharnaces killed", "summary": "Pharnaces fled to the Bosporus and was killed by his governor Asander. Caesar gave the Bosporus to Mithridates of Pergamon, freed Amisos, and adjusted the client states, reducing Deiotarus.", "people": ["Julius Caesar", "Pharnaces II", "Asander", "Mithridates of Pergamon", "Deiotarus"], "sources_primary": ["Bellum Alexandrinum 77-78", "Appian, Mith. 120-121", "Cassius Dio 42.47.5-48.4", "Strabo 13.4.3"], "sources_modern": ["Sullivan 1990"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Pantikapaion", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "Pharnaces killed; Bosporus to Mithridates of Pergamon"}, {"name": "Amisos", "lat": 41.3, "lon": 36.34, "role": "declared free"}]}, {"id": "H2-073", "date": "-45", "date_text": "46/45 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Colonia Iulia Felix Sinope", "summary": "Caesar settled Roman colonists at Sinope, which became Colonia Iulia Felix Sinope; the colony's coins count an era from 45 BC. Strabo notes the Roman colony beside the Greek city.", "people": ["Julius Caesar"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 12.3.11", "Colonial coins of Sinope"], "sources_modern": ["Marek 1993", "Mitchell 1993"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Founded between 47 and 45; the colony's coins count an era from 45 BC.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "Roman colony"}]}, {"id": "H2-074", "date": "-39", "date_text": "39 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Antony gives Pontus to Darius son of Pharnaces", "summary": "Mark Antony, reorganising the east, gave Pontus to Darius, a son of Pharnaces II and grandson of Mithridates VI. The Mithridatid line thus briefly returned.", "people": ["Mark Antony", "Darius son of Pharnaces"], "sources_primary": ["Appian, Civil Wars 5.75"], "sources_modern": ["Sullivan 1980 (ANRW II.7.2)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The extent of Darius' kingdom is uncertain.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Pontus (interior)", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "kingdom of Darius"}]}, {"id": "H2-075", "date": "-37", "date_text": "37/36 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Polemon I king of Pontus", "summary": "Antony made Polemon, son of the orator Zeno of Laodiceia, king of Pontus east of the Halys and later added Lesser Armenia. Polemon survived Antony's fall and was confirmed by Augustus as a friend and ally of the Roman people; the Polemonid kingdom held the eastern coast, including Trapezous and Pharnakeia, until AD 64.", "people": ["Polemon I", "Mark Antony", "Augustus", "Zeno of Laodiceia"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 12.8.16; 14.2.24", "Cassius Dio 49.25.4, 49.33.2, 49.44.3; 53.25.1"], "sources_modern": ["Sullivan 1980", "Mitchell 1993"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "When Polemonion, the older Side, took its name is not known.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kabeira region", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.955, "role": "core of the kingdom"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.727, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Pharnakeia", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Polemonion (Bolaman near Fatsa, approximate)", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 37.52, "role": "town named after the dynasty"}]}, {"id": "H2-076", "date": "-36", "date_text": "36 BC", "precision": "decade", "title": "Borders 36 BC: Antony's client kingdoms", "summary": "Under Antony: Polemon I held Pontus east of the Halys and Iris (Kabeira, Pharnakeia, Trapezous) with Lesser Armenia. Inner Pontus around Amaseia and Gaziura under local dynasts ('given to kings' in Strabo's words). The coastal west (Sinope, Amisos, Amastris) remained in the province of Bithynia et Pontus, with Amisos briefly under a tyrant.", "people": ["Polemon I", "Mark Antony"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 12.3.14, 12.3.38-39", "Cassius Dio 49.25, 49.33"], "sources_modern": ["Sullivan 1980", "Marek 1993"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "Internal boundaries are uncertain.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kabeira", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.955, "role": "Polemon"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.727, "role": "Polemon"}, {"name": "Lesser Armenia", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 38.12, "role": "Polemon"}, {"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "dynast"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "province"}, {"name": "Amisos", "lat": 41.3, "lon": 36.34, "role": "province / tyrant"}]}, {"id": "H2-077", "date": "-14", "date_text": "14 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Polemon I given the Bosporus", "summary": "Agrippa gave Polemon the Bosporan kingdom; he married its queen Dynamis, granddaughter of Mithridates VI. For a few years one king ruled both Pontus and the Kerch strait again.", "people": ["Polemon I", "Agrippa", "Dynamis"], "sources_primary": ["Cassius Dio 54.24.4-6"], "sources_modern": ["Sullivan 1980"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Pantikapaion", "lat": 45.35, "lon": 36.47, "role": "Bosporan capital"}]}, {"id": "H2-078", "date": "-8", "date_text": "C. 8 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Polemon killed; Pythodoris queen of Pontus", "summary": "Polemon died fighting the Aspurgians near Sindike. His widow Pythodoris, daughter of Pythodoros of Tralles and through her mother a granddaughter of Mark Antony, ruled Pontus. She later married Archelaus of Cappadocia; Strabo, writing in her lifetime, calls her wise and able and lists her lands: Pharnakeia and Trapezous, the Tibarenoi and Chaldaioi up to Colchis, and the Kabeira district, which she made her residence under the name Sebaste.", "people": ["Pythodoris", "Polemon I", "Archelaus of Cappadocia", "Polemon II", "Zeno-Artaxias III"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 11.2.11; 12.3.29; 12.3.31", "Inscriptions naming Pythodoris and her Antonian descent"], "sources_modern": ["Sullivan 1980", "Dueck 2000"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The end of her reign is unknown (after AD 21; some lists run it to 38).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Aspurgian country (Kuban, approximate)", "lat": 45.1, "lon": 37.8, "role": "Polemon's death"}, {"name": "Sebaste (Kabeira)", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.955, "role": "royal residence"}, {"name": "Pharnakeia", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "in her realm"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.727, "role": "in her realm"}]}, {"id": "H2-079", "date": "-8", "date_text": "C. 8 BC-AD 23", "precision": "decade", "title": "Borders c. 8 BC-AD 20: Pythodoris' Pontus", "summary": "From Strabo (12.3.29–31): Queen Pythodoris held the Kabeira-Sebaste district, Phanaroia, Pharnakeia, Trapezous, the Tibarenoi and Chaldaioi as far as Colchis, and Polemonion. Inner Pontus (Amaseia, Sebastopolis, Megalopolis-Sebasteia) passed to Rome in 3/2 BC; Komana stayed a temple-state until 34/35.", "people": ["Pythodoris"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 12.3.29-31, 12.3.37"], "sources_modern": ["Sullivan 1980", "Marek 1993"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sebaste", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.955, "role": "inside (residence)"}, {"name": "Pharnakeia", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.727, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Polemonion", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 37.52, "role": "inside"}, {"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "outside from 3/2 BC (Galatia)"}, {"name": "Komana", "lat": 40.355, "lon": 36.635, "role": "temple-state"}]}, {"id": "H2-080", "date": "-3", "date_text": "3/2 BC", "precision": "year", "title": "Pontus Galaticus annexed to Galatia", "summary": "Inner Pontus around Amaseia, with Sebastopolis and Megalopolis-Sebasteia, was annexed and attached to the province of Galatia; the cities' coins count their era from 3/2 BC. The area came to be called Pontus Galaticus.", "people": ["Augustus"], "sources_primary": ["City coin eras", "Ptolemy, Geography 5.6.3-4 (Pontus Galaticus)"], "sources_modern": ["Marek 1993", "Mitchell 1993, vol. 1"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Which cities joined in 3/2 BC and which later is debated; Zela's status is unclear.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "annexed; era 3/2 BC"}, {"name": "Sebastopolis (Sulusaray)", "lat": 40.0, "lon": 36.08, "role": "annexed"}, {"name": "Sebasteia (Sivas)", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.015, "role": "annexed"}]}, {"id": "H2-081", "date": "17", "date_text": "AD 17", "precision": "year", "title": "Death of Archelaus; Cappadocia a province", "summary": "Archelaus of Cappadocia, Pythodoris' second husband, died in Rome and Tiberius annexed Cappadocia. Pythodoris kept her Pontic kingdom.", "people": ["Archelaus of Cappadocia", "Tiberius", "Pythodoris"], "sources_primary": ["Tacitus, Annals 2.42", "Strabo 12.1.4"], "sources_modern": ["Mitchell 1993"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Caesarea-Mazaka", "lat": 38.72, "lon": 35.49, "role": "capital of the new province"}]}, {"id": "H2-082", "date": "18", "date_end": "24", "date_text": "C. AD 18-24", "precision": "decade", "title": "Strabo of Amaseia describes his homeland (Geography book 12)", "summary": "Strabo, born at Amaseia c. 64 BC into a Greek family of the Mithridatic court, wrote the Pontic part of his Geography under Tiberius. He describes 'our city' in the Iris gorge with the palaces and 'memorials of the kings', the iron mines of the Chaldaioi, Kabeira's palace, water-mill and game parks, the temple-states of Komana ('not fewer than 6,000' temple servants, 12.3.34) and Zela, the plain of Themiskyra, and the 'maddening honey'. It is the only eyewitness description of Pontus from antiquity.", "people": ["Strabo of Amaseia"], "sources_primary": ["Strabo 12.3.1-41 (esp. 12.3.11, 12.3.14-19, 12.3.28-41)", "Strabo 10.4.10 (family)"], "sources_modern": ["Dueck 2000", "Roller 2018 (Commentary)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Most of it was written about AD 18–24: it mentions Zeno-Artaxias' accession (18) and Archelaus' death (17).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "Strabo's birthplace"}, {"name": "Kabeira", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.955, "role": "described"}, {"name": "Komana", "lat": 40.355, "lon": 36.635, "role": "described"}, {"name": "Zela", "lat": 40.303, "lon": 35.886, "role": "described"}, {"name": "Themiskyra", "lat": 41.208, "lon": 36.972, "role": "described"}, {"name": "Sinope", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.153, "role": "described"}]}, {"id": "H2-083", "date": "34", "date_text": "AD 34/35", "precision": "year", "title": "Komana Pontica annexed", "summary": "The temple-state of Komana Pontica, ruled by its priest-dynasts since Pompey, was absorbed into the province; its coins count an era from 34/35.", "people": ["Tiberius"], "sources_primary": ["City coin era"], "sources_modern": ["Marek 1993"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Komana Pontica", "lat": 40.355, "lon": 36.635, "role": "annexed"}]}, {"id": "H2-084", "date": "38", "date_text": "AD 38", "precision": "year", "title": "Polemon II king of Pontus", "summary": "Caligula made Polemon II, son of Polemon I and Pythodoris, king of Pontus; in 41 Claudius gave the Bosporus to another dynast and compensated Polemon with land in Cilicia.", "people": ["Polemon II", "Caligula", "Claudius"], "sources_primary": ["Cassius Dio 59.12.2; 60.8.2"], "sources_modern": ["Sullivan 1980"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sebaste (Kabeira)", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.955, "role": "royal seat (probable)"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.727, "role": "in the kingdom"}]}], "turkish": [], "synthesis": "## A Persian dynasty on a Greek coast\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch03_pontus.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch03_pontus.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1080\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the kingdom of Pontus from Amastris to Trapezous, showing its cities, the cities founded by Pompey and the battles of Amnias, the Halys and Zela.\"></a><figcaption>The kingdom of Pontus: its cities, the battles fought in it, and the cities Pompey founded in 64–63 BC. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nIn 302 BC Antigonus the One-Eyed killed Mithridates, the Persian dynast of Kios on the Sea of Marmara. A young kinsman of the same name, a companion of Antigonus' son Demetrius, escaped east \"with six horsemen\" (Appian, *Mithridatic Wars* 9) and seized Kimiata, a stronghold at the foot of the Olgassys, the Ilgaz range (Strabo 12.3.41). From there he made himself master of \"Cappadocia by the Sea\", the country the Greeks came to call simply Pontus, and about 281 he took the title of king; his descendants called him *Ktistes*, \"the Founder\". The kingdom's beginning can be dated to 302, the flight; to 297/6, the start of the royal era used on the later coins; or to 281, the royal title, which agrees with Syncellus' 218 years for the Pontic kings. 281 is the date usually given, but all three have their defenders (H2-001–003).\n\nThe kings were Mithridates I (to 266), Ariobarzanes (about 266–250), Mithridates II (about 250–220), a Mithridates III known mainly from coins (about 220–185), Pharnaces I (about 189/185 to 160/155), Mithridates IV with his sister-queen Laodice (to about 150), Mithridates V Euergetes (about 150–120) and Mithridates VI Eupator (120–63). Their first royal city was Amaseia, a rock fortress in the gorge of the Iris. Strabo, who was born there, describes \"the palaces and memorials of the kings\" inside its walls (12.3.39); the five great rock-cut tombs in the Harşena cliff are the visible remains, usually given to the kings who reigned before the court moved to Sinope.\n\n## Sinope and Pharnakeia\n\nFor a century the kingdom was an inland power. Its only Greek port was Amastris, handed to Ariobarzanes about 279 (Memnon F 9.4). Mithridates II besieged Sinope in 220 and failed (Polybius 4.56). In 183 Pharnaces I took it: Strabo says the city was \"taken by siege\" and was \"subject first to Pharnaces\" (12.3.11). Sinope later became the royal city: Mithridates VI was born and raised there and, in Strabo's words, treated it as \"the metropolis of the kingdom\", though a move of the court there as early as 183 is a modern inference. Pharnaces also founded Pharnakeia, moving the people of Kotyora into it; Arrian says it was \"formerly called Kerasous\" (*Periplus* 16), and it is the Kerasous of later centuries, today's Giresun.\n\nThe four-year war that followed, from 183 to 179, set Pharnaces and his ally in Lesser Armenia against Pergamon, Cappadocia and Bithynia. It ended in the treaty Polybius quotes (25.2): Pharnaces gave up Galatia and Paphlagonia and paid heavily, but kept Sinope. The treaty names Chersonesos in the Crimea among the cities included in the peace, and a separate oath of alliance with Chersonesos (IOSPE I² 402) already points north across the sea.\n\n## Mithridates VI Eupator\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch03_empire.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch03_empire.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1400\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the Black Sea and Asia Minor showing the lands of Mithridates VI around the sea, his conquests of 88 BC, the battles and sieges of his wars with Rome, and his flight to Pantikapaion.\"></a><figcaption>The Black Sea empire of Mithridates VI: Pontus, Lesser Armenia, Colchis and the Bosporan kingdom, his conquests of 88 BC, lost by 85, the main battles and sieges of his wars with Rome, and his flight of 66–65 BC. The areas are drawn approximately. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nMithridates VI was born at Sinope about 134/132, succeeded as a boy in 120 and ruled alone from about 115. He turned the kingdom into an empire round the Black Sea. The ruler of Lesser Armenia, Antipater, ceded his land, where, Strabo says, the king built 75 treasure-forts (12.3.28). Colchis was ruled through governors, one of them Strabo's great-uncle Moaphernes. In the Crimea his general Diophantus of Sinope saved Chersonesos from the Scythians and put down a revolt at Pantikapaion, and the Bosporan kingdom became Pontic, about 110–107 (the Diophantus decree, IOSPE I² 352). When he pushed on into Paphlagonia, Galatia and Cappadocia, he came into conflict with Rome.\n\n**The First Mithridatic War, 89–85.** In 89, urged on by the Roman legate Manius Aquillius, Nicomedes IV of Bithynia raided Pontus. Mithridates' generals destroyed the Bithynian army on the Amnias, Rome's improvised armies scattered, and the province of Asia welcomed the king; Aquillius was killed at Pergamon with molten gold. In 88 Mithridates ordered what is called the \"Asian Vespers\": on a fixed day the Romans and Italians in every city were to be killed (Appian 22–23). The ancient figures for the dead run from 80,000 (Memnon; Valerius Maximus) to 150,000 (Plutarch, *Sulla* 24.4), and many modern historians think the real number much lower. His fleets sacked Delos, where 20,000 were killed, mostly Italians (Appian 28), and Athens joined him. Sulla took Athens on 1 March 86 and destroyed Pontic armies at Chaeronea (86) and Orchomenos (85), while the renegade Roman Fimbria nearly caught the king at Pitane. The Peace of Dardanus (85) cost Mithridates all his conquests and an indemnity (2,000 talents and 70 ships in Plutarch; 3,000 talents and 80 triremes in Memnon), but left him his kingdom.\n\n**The Second War, 83–81,** was a sideshow: Murena raided Komana and was beaten on the Halys in 82.\n\n**The Third War, 73–63,** began when the last king of Bithynia left his kingdom to Rome. Mithridates beat Cotta at Chalcedon but wrecked his own army before Cyzicus (73–72). Lucullus then invaded Pontus. After the collapse at Kabeira in the winter of 72/71 the king fled to his son-in-law Tigranes in Armenia, having first had his sisters and his Greek wives killed at Pharnakeia. Amisos fell in 71, burned by its own defender and rebuilt by Lucullus; Sinope fell in 70, and Amaseia surrendered. Lucullus beat Tigranes at Tigranocerta on 6 October 69, but his army mutinied, and Mithridates came back and killed 7,000 Romans, among them 24 tribunes and 150 centurions, at Zela in 67 (Appian 89; Plutarch, *Lucullus* 35). Pompey, given an extraordinary command in 66, caught him in a night battle in Lesser Armenia and founded Nicopolis on the battlefield. The old king fled to Colchis and in 65 went round the Caucasus coast to the Bosporus. There, in 63, his son Pharnaces turned the army against him, and he had himself killed at Pantikapaion, after a reign of 57 years, aged 68 or 69 (Appian 112). Pompey buried him \"among the kings\" at Sinope. Pliny says he ruled twenty-two peoples and spoke all their languages (*Natural History* 7.88, 25.6).\n\n## After Mithridates\n\nPompey joined western Pontus to Bithynia as one province, divided into eleven city territories, founded Nicopolis, Magnopolis, Diospolis (Kabeira), Neapolis, Megalopolis and Pompeiopolis, and gave the east to client kings. His inscription to Minerva claimed 12,183,000 people routed, killed or received in surrender, 846 ships taken and 1,538 towns and forts (Pliny, *Natural History* 7.97), and the placards of his triumph added 39 cities founded (Plutarch, *Pompey* 45). In 48–47 Pharnaces II tried to win back his father's kingdom. He beat Domitius Calvinus at Nicopolis and sacked Amisos, but Caesar routed him at Zela on 2 August 47 (the day is from the Fasti Amiternini) and summed it up: \"veni, vidi, vici\".\n\nAntony gave Pontus first to a grandson of Mithridates, Darius (39), then to Polemon of Laodiceia (37/36). After Polemon was killed about 8 BC his widow Pythodoris, a granddaughter of Mark Antony, ruled the kingdom; Strabo, writing in her lifetime, calls her wise and able (H2-078). Their son Polemon II held it from AD 38 to 64. This Polemonid kingdom kept the eastern coast with Trapezous and Pharnakeia, while inner Pontus around Amaseia was annexed to the empire piece by piece (3/2 BC; Komana in AD 34/35).\n\n## The Greek court of an Iranian dynasty\n\nThe Mithridatids were Persian by descent and proud of it. Mithridates VI claimed descent from Cyrus and Darius on his father's side and from Alexander and Seleucus on his mother's (Justin 38.7.1; Appian 112), and he sacrificed to Zeus Stratios on mountain-tops in the Persian manner (Appian 66, 70). Iranian cults ran through the kingdom: Anaitis at Zela (Strabo 12.3.37), Men \"of Pharnaces\" at Ameria, by whom the royal oath was sworn (12.3.31), and later Mithras at Trapezous. Komana Pontica, a temple-state, had not fewer than 6,000 temple servants (12.3.34).\n\nThe court and the state were nevertheless Greek in language and form. The king's generals and ministers were mostly Greeks from the Pontic cities and the Aegean: Diophantus of Sinope, Archelaus, Neoptolemus, Dorylaus, and his chief physician, Papias of Amisos, who appear on the monument Helianax built for him on Delos in 102/1. His coins carry a portrait in the manner of Alexander and Greek legends, and dozens of Pontic towns, inland ones among them, struck Greek civic bronzes. Several of his wives were Greeks, such as Monime of Miletus and Berenice of Chios, and in 88 he presented himself to the Greeks as their liberator from Rome. Strabo's own family shows the mixture: his mother's relatives bore Greek names (Dorylaus, Lagetas, Theophilus), an Iranian one (Moaphernes) and an Anatolian one (Tibius), and served the king as generals, priests and governors (10.4.10; 12.3.33).\n\nThe kingdom's twenty-two peoples included the Greek coastal cities, Amastris, Sinope, Amisos, Kotyora, Pharnakeia-Kerasous and Trapezous; the Paphlagonians in the west; the \"White Syrians\", or Cappadocians, in the interior; the mountain peoples of the east, the Chaldaioi (the old Chalybes, miners), the Tibarenoi and the Heptakometai (Xenophon's Mossynoikoi); the Armenians of Lesser Armenia; the Colchians on the Phasis; and the Scythians, Sarmatians and Greek Bosporans of the north. The Galatians, the Celts of central Anatolia, were neighbours, mercenaries and enemies. Under the kings Greek was the language of the court, of the army's command and of the mints, and Pompey's foundations were organised as Greek cities: the root of the Greek-speaking Pontus of the later chapters.\n\n## The numbers, and who gives them\n\n| What | Figure | Who gives it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dead in the \"Asian Vespers\", 88 BC | 80,000; 150,000 | Memnon and Valerius Maximus; Plutarch (many think both too high) |\n| Killed at Delos, 88 BC | 20,000, mostly Italians | Appian 28 |\n| Pontic forces, 89 BC | 250,000 foot, 40,000 horse, 300 decked ships | Appian 17; not credible as given |\n| Chaeronea, 86 BC | a Pontic army of 110,000–120,000, of whom 10,000 survived; 14 or 15 Romans missing, 2 of whom came back | Plutarch, *Sulla*; Appian |\n| Tigranocerta, 69 BC | Tigranes' 250,000 against Lucullus' 11,000; 5 Roman dead | Plutarch, *Lucullus* 26–28; propaganda |\n| Zela, 67 BC | 7,000 Roman dead | Appian 89; Plutarch, *Lucullus* 35 |\n| Nicopolis, 66 BC | Pontic dead \"many more than 10,000\" to 40,000 killed or taken; about 40 Romans killed | Plutarch, *Pompey* 32.7; Orosius 6.4.5 |\n\nNo ancient source gives a population for the kingdom.\n\n## Where historians disagree\n\nDiodorus and Plutarch disagree about the founder's parentage, and the story of the warning \"Flee, Mithridates\" is court legend (Bosworth & Wheatley 1998). Whether Mithridates III existed rests on coins, and the numbering of the kings depends on it. How many died in the Vespers, and whether the Greek townsmen killed willingly, as Appian implies, or under orders, is disputed. So is Mithridates himself: a Hellenistic liberator, as he presented himself, or the oriental despot of Roman propaganda (McGing 1986; Mayor 2010). The site of Tigranocerta (Silvan, Arzan or the Garzan valley), the identity of Pompey's eleven cities and the eastern limit of his province (Marek 1993; Madsen 2009), and whether the Third War began in 74 or 73 are all debated.\n\n## Legend and evidence\n\nAntigonus' dream, Mithridates' years hiding in the forest, his immunity to poison and his antidote, the \"mithridatium\", the bees and bears at Themiskyra, and Lucullus' dream of Autolykos are legend or romance. But the comets said to have marked his birth and accession may match comets in the Chinese records for 135 and 119 BC (Ramsey 1999), and Hypsikrateia, the concubine who rode with him in men's clothes, seems to be confirmed by an epitaph found at Phanagoria in 2004 (Bernard 2006; Gabelko 2013). The strongest evidence comes from inscriptions, the Diophantus decree, the treaty of 179 and the monuments on Delos, and from the dated coins.\n\n## The sources\n\nAppian's *Mithridatica* is the fullest narrative of the kingdom and its wars with Rome. Plutarch's lives of Sulla, Lucullus, Pompey and Caesar tell the wars from the Roman commanders' side, drawing on Sulla's memoirs, Sallust and Theophanes. Memnon of Heraclea, surviving in Photius' summary, gives the history of Heraclea and its neighbours, with the early kings and the Third War. Strabo, book 12, describes the kingdom's lands, cities and temples, and his own family's service to the kings. Polybius (books 23–25) gives Pharnaces' war and quotes the treaty of 179. Justin keeps Mithridates' speech and the story of his youth. Cassius Dio covers Pompey and Pharnaces II, and the *Alexandrian War* Caesar's campaign of 47. Among modern works, McGing (1986) is the standard study of Mithridates VI; Højte (2009) gathers current research; Mayor (2010) is a full biography; Sherwin-White (1984) and Magie (1950) give the Roman side; Keaveney (1992) and Seager (2002) the lives of Lucullus and Pompey; Sullivan (1980, 1990) the client dynasts; Erciyas (2006) the archaeology and royal ideology of the Mithridatids; and Dueck (2000) and Roller (2018) Strabo.\n"};
